red over to break down the waste  products  what you're left with is a clear  solution of monomers  these are the building blocks that you  would originally get from oil and gas  and they're exactly the same  except this time you can do it over and  over again in  infinite circular economy style and this  is where we want to get to  currently the enzymes are too slow to be  economically viable you're dealing with  pet plastic but  there's plenty of other types aren't  there so  does that complicate recycling pet is  around maybe 20  of the plastic out there but we also  have lots of other types of plastic  polyethylene plastic bags polypropylene  for  for harder plastics pvc many many  different types  and of course this is the problem if  you're a recycling facility  you need a way to sort through these and  pull out value for from these  materials and that's a real challenge  and this is what we're struggling with  we tend to demonize plastics but  actually it's not  how we use them it's how we dispose of  them that's the real issue  we've never been that good at recycling  even before the pandemic  when major corporations like danone and  coca-cola were pledging to increase  recycled plastics in their packaging  only about nine percent of all plastic  ever produced has been made into  something new  it's rather disconcerting that less than  10 of plastic bottles are actually  recycled back into plastic bottles  part of the reason for that is economics  the falling price of oil has compounded  the problem  it's meant it's become much cheaper to  make brand new plastic  so manufacturers have less incentive to  use recycled materials  companies really want to use recycled  material but it's expensive and it's  often a poor quality  that's because if you mechanically  recycle pet or other plastics  if you've got any contaminants colors  and things basically the  material loses its material properties  each time you do it  so even though people when they put  those bo